[Music]  the city of buffalo is mourning the 10  people who were gunned down at a grocery  store in a predominantly black  neighborhood three others were hurt and  one of those killed was 86 year old ruth  whitfield she's a mother she's a wife  and she's a grandmother her son garnell  whitfield is a retired commissioner of  the buffalo fire department he joins us  along with attorney ben crump who is  representing the whitfield family good  morning to you both  mr whitfield i want to start with you  because we are also very sorry that you  and any other family is going through  this pain this morning but i'm curious  it's been reported that you went to the  store to help and once you arrived you  learned that your mother was in fact one  of the victims how did you find out that  you had now been personally affected by  this  my mom had gone to the nursing home  which he did every day for the last  eight years to uh care for my father who  was at the nursing home  and uh she didn't answer her phone and  the nursing home is just blocks uh from  the scene here uh upon her not asking  her phone uh just walked the scene here  and was able to ascertain that her  vehicle was uh in the lot here at this  store  garnell you mentioned your mother  visiting your father in the nursing home  tell us a little bit more about their  relationship  my  my parents were married for 67 68 years  we were wonderfully blessed to have uh  have them  all at this time  my father had taken neal and had  been  in this nursing home for the last eight  years  my mom  was there every day to take care of my  father every day she loved them  completely and she was doing what she  did every day  and she left there and stopped by the  store on the way home to get groceries  and encountered this individual  and ben i would like to ask you about  the representation of the ruth  ruth whitfield family in a statement  yesterday you said we are thoroughly  investigating the shooting and the  events leading up to it  what further answers do you want to know  well nate it's important that we not  only hold accountable  this individual who committed this  hateful act but we hold accountable  those who curate this hate who  radicalized these young white  supremacists i mean that manifesto he  said his intent was to kill  as many black people as he could that  day we saw in charleston south carolina  with the young white supremacist dylan  roof and now we're here in buffalo new  york how long before we get the  anti-black hate crime bill passed  because if we don't do something  meaningful nate and gail  i am so afraid that we will see  something like this happen again in our  communities  i i think i think ben trump were all  afraid of that you know it doesn't seem  like you're safe at the grocery store  the church at the spa at a movie theater  at a concert and now we have a man in  custody what do you make of the arrest  of this young man  you know  gail so many times we talk about unarmed  young people of color being killed but  yet when you have these  young  confirmed white mass murderers they  always seem to be taken alive  and so it just  goes to a deeper conversation  of uh how we deal with societal issues  especially involving race we have to  direct our attention to these internet  sites that inspire these young people  that are radicalizing them to be  hate-mongers to be people who hate  people because the color of their skin  it is very hard ben to hear that this  young man was so young and had so much  hatred in his heart  i want to go back to you mr whitfield  because you know i think about the 10  families who are waking up today whose  lives are forever changed i'm wondering  how you're processing this loss yourself  it's very difficult this is surreal we  we are  we are in a place that we never expected  to be  um my mother was the glue that held us  together my mother was my father's  caretaker  we've not shared this information with  my father we don't know if that's in his  best interest we we don't know how to  proceed uh we have to consult with his  caretakers with his doctors and find out  uh you know how to proceed with this we  don't know if he should attend the  memorial service for my father we don't  know how we're going to care for him  going forward in a manner consistent  with what my mother uh would have wanted  and so we are  we have a tough a tough road ahead it's  difficult days i think not just for your  family but for the country there's some  very difficult questions we all need to  face and we all need to answer we thank  you both thank you both

